Q: Can&#39;t synthesize when using generate block A: <p>Please keep in mind that Verilog is a hardware description language and does not work the same way as programming languages. It helps to think in terms of hardware as wires, counters and parallel execution, and not in terms of variables and sequential execution.</p>
<p><code>genvar for</code> loops are not intended for the same operation as in programming languages. The synthesis tool unrolls the for loop and hardcodes the parameters and <code>genvar</code> indices.</p>
<p>The correct way to assign <code>squares</code> using <code>genvar</code> to replicate the squares is shown below.</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>localparam n = 5;
localparam blockSize = 20;

localparam rowStartCol = 144;
localparam colStartRow = 35;

wire [9:0] squares[n:0][n:0][1:0];

genvar i, j;
generate
    for (i = 0 ; i &lt;= n ; i = i + 1) begin
        for (j = 0 ; j &lt;= n; j = j + 1) begin

            assign square[i][j][0] = rowStartCol + i*blockSize;
            assign square[i][j][1] = colStartRow + j*blockSize; 

        end
    end
endgenerate
</code></pre>

Q: no match for call to &#39;(student) (std::string&amp;, int&amp;, int&amp;)&#39; A: <p>It looks like you intend to call the constructor of class <code>student</code>. The way to do that in your original code with the non-standard variable-length array is to assign a newly constructed object to the existing object</p>
<pre><code>students[i] = student(a, b, c);
</code></pre>
<p>or, since the constructor is not <code>explicit</code>, using an assignment from a brace initializer</p>
<pre><code>students[i] = {a, b, c};
</code></pre>
<p>However, you should actually use a <code>std::vector</code> in place of the variable-length array</p>
<pre><code>std::vector&lt;student&gt; students;
</code></pre>
<p>and then you can construct the new object in place without an intermediate, temporary object:</p>
<pre><code>students.emplace(a, b, c);
</code></pre>
